{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nThe operation-linked compaction sweep is not crash-atomic, and this is the root wedge-generator for the store (parent epic becoming-berserk-grunion; live wedges recorded on e72e-259d-5ee7-4e73 and 6ccc-0577-198c-44fa). rebar._commands.compact_trigger.maybe_compact() fires on the tail of every ticket CLOSE and spawns a DETACHED sweep worker (run_sweep → compact.compact_all_cli → compact_txn). The fold performs snapshot-write → retire-rename (tracked source → `*.retired`) → git commit as SEPARATE steps with no rollback. If the detached worker dies, is killed, or races a concurrent writer between the rename and the commit, the SHARED tracker working tree is left DIRTY: tracked deletions plus untracked `*-SNAPSHOT.json` / `*.retired` files. A dirty tree then aborts the next reconverge union merge (\"local changes / untracked files would be overwritten\"), stranding local ticket commits — the chronic wedge. Interim mitigation `[compact] trigger=\"off\"` is active on the affected host and must be removable once this lands.\n\n## Approach\nMake the fold crash-atomic with an intent journal + rollback + startup recovery (the classic write-ahead-intent pattern):\n1. **Intent journal**: before mutating anything, `_apply_fold` writes a journal record (ticket dir, snapshot path, sources to retire) to a store-local journal location; if the journal cannot be written, the fold REFUSES (logs, stderr Error, exit 1) without mutating the store.\n2. **Ordered mutation + rollback**: perform snapshot-write, then renames, then ONE commit; on any failure after mutation begins, `_rollback_fold` reverse-renames and removes the uncommitted snapshot. If a reverse-rename itself fails (incomplete rollback), RETAIN the uncommitted snapshot rather than delete it — dropping it would lose folded events; the retained state is exactly what fsck reports as SNAPSHOT_INCONSISTENT and doctor can repair.\n3. **Recovery**: compact_recovery.recover_abandoned_folds scans for journals with no matching commit at startup/next sweep entry, discriminating: journal + uncommitted artifacts present → revert (restore sources, remove snapshot, clear journal); journal whose snapshot already reached HEAD → clear journal (fold landed); journal whose snapshot is gone from disk (superseded or fsck-repaired) → clear journal without touching ticket files.\n4. Transient-fault discipline: git probes used for discrimination must distinguish \"object absent\" from transient failures (index.lock contention, timeout) so recovery never mis-classifies a transient fault as \"snapshot absent\" and reverts a landed fold.\nThe journal lives with the store and is cleaned in the same locked scope as the fold; the sweep remains operation-linked (portable — no CI dependence).\n\n## Scope\n- src/rebar/_commands/compact_txn.py — intent journal write, ordered fold, `_rollback_fold` with the retain-on-incomplete-rollback guard.\n- src/rebar/_commands/compact_recovery.py — abandoned-fold discrimination + recovery.\n- src/rebar/_commands/compact.py — recovery invocation at sweep entry.\n- tests/unit/test_compact_crash_atomicity.py (new) — kill-at-each-step coverage.\n- tests/unit/test_lock_holder_labelling.py — lock label update.\n\n## Testing\n- Crash injection at each step boundary (after journal, after snapshot-write, after renames, before commit): the next sweep entry recovers to a clean tree (`git status --porcelain` empty) with no ticket events lost.\n- Refusal path: journal write failure → exit 1, store untouched (zero mutations, zero commits).\n- Incomplete rollback: a failing reverse-rename retains the uncommitted snapshot; assert the snapshot bytes survive and fsck reports the state.\n- Recovery discrimination: landed-fold journal is cleared without reverting; superseded/fsck-repaired journal (snapshot gone from disk) is cleared without touching ticket files; transient git failure during the probe does NOT trigger a revert.\n- Idempotence: recovery on a clean store makes zero changes; double recovery is safe.\n- Manual mutation pass over journal/rollback/recovery branches; every mutant killed.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A fold killed at ANY step boundary leaves a store that the next sweep entry converges to clean (no dirty tracked deletions, no stray snapshots, no lost events); checked by the crash-injection tests.\n- [ ] A fold that cannot journal refuses without mutating the store; checked by the refusal test.\n- [ ] Recovery never deletes folded-event bytes: incomplete rollback retains the snapshot, and landed/superseded journals are cleared without reverting ticket files; checked by the discrimination tests.\n- [ ] Transient git faults during recovery probes are not treated as \"snapshot absent\"; checked by the fault-injection test.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` clean; lands via Gerrit (LLM-Review +1 AND Verified +1) before close; after merge the `[compact] trigger=\"off\"` mitigation is removed on the affected host."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786686547706429001,"uuid":"09a3b9a3-77dd-4ad9-aba8-50e48c19c958"}